From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: ntdll, kernelbase: a user's network drives and UNC paths

Windows reaches \\server\share\... directly, connecting on first use, and a
drive letter mapped to a share (a home drive, a logon script's NET USE, a
Group Policy drive map) belongs to that logon session alone. Wine resolves
drive letters and UNC paths only through the prefix's dosdevices, which a
shared prefix shares between every user.

Stained Glass OS mounts shares with the user's own Kerberos ticket
(sg-netmountd in sg-session: kernel CIFS, "multiuser", so the file server
checks each user) under /run/stained-glass-net/unc/<server>/<share>, and
keeps a user's drive letters as symlinks in
/run/stained-glass-net/drives/<uid>/.

- A drive letter is looked up in the user's own directory first, as Windows
  consults a session's DosDevices before the global one; the list is
  rescanned once a second, and at once when a letter the prefix lacks too is
  not found (a program that maps a drive and uses it immediately).
- \??\UNC\server\share\... resolves under /run/stained-glass-net/unc when
  the share is mounted there; otherwise ntdll asks sg-netmountd over its
  socket to mount it, and remembers a failure for ten seconds so that
  programs probing paths do not hammer the server.
- The reverse mapping (a Unix path back to a drive) knows the user's drives.
- GetLogicalDrives reports the user's letters, which are not in the
  machine's DosDevices directory.

Nothing changes when /run/stained-glass-net does not exist.

